INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinning mill''s technical team debates whether to install an ''autoleveller'' (a device on the draw frame that continuously measures sliver thickness and automatically adjusts draft in real time to correct thickness variation) versus relying purely on good upstream process control. What SPECIFIC type of variation does an autoleveller correct that upstream process control alone cannot fully address?', '["Autolevellers have no real function and cannot correct any type of sliver variation, making the entire technology a non-functional marketing concept","An autoleveller corrects SHORT-TERM, RANDOM variation in sliver mass that arises from inherent statistical fiber-packing randomness (the same underlying randomness responsible for baseline CV%, as established earlier) — this random, unpredictable variation cannot be eliminated by upstream process control alone (since it''s not caused by any correctable systematic machine fault), but CAN be actively measured and corrected in real time by continuously adjusting draft to compensate for each detected thickness fluctuation","Autolevellers can only correct LONG-TERM systematic trends occurring over many days, with zero capability to address any short-term random variation","Upstream process control alone can always achieve mathematically perfect zero sliver variation, making autolevellers entirely redundant technology with no genuine additional benefit"]'::jsonb, 'An autoleveller corrects SHORT-TERM, RANDOM variation in sliver mass that arises from inherent statistical fiber-packing randomness (the same underlying randomness responsible for baseline CV%, as established earlier) — this random, unpredictable variation cannot be eliminated by upstream process control alone (since it''s not caused by any correctable systematic machine fault), but CAN be actively measured and corrected in real time by continuously adjusting draft to compensate for each detected thickness fluctuation', 'This connects to the earlier baseline-CV%-is-unavoidable discussion (even a perfectly functioning process has SOME inherent random mass variation from statistical fiber-packing randomness) — since this random component isn''t caused by any single correctable systematic fault, upstream process control (fixing machine settings, roller alignment, etc.) can''t eliminate it entirely; an autoleveller instead takes an ACTIVE, real-time CORRECTIVE approach — continuously measuring actual sliver thickness and adjusting draft moment-to-moment to compensate for each detected fluctuation, directly reducing the FINAL sliver''s evenness variation regardless of its random or systematic origin, a fundamentally different (active correction) approach than passive upstream process optimization alone.', 3, '{"autoleveller-function"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A ring-spinning mill''s technical team observes that switching from a standard steel traveller to a ceramic-coated traveller (for the SAME weight/shape) REDUCES yarn hairiness measurably, even though traveller weight (which controls tension, as established earlier) is unchanged. What property of the ceramic coating most directly explains this hairiness reduction?', '["Ceramic coating has no relationship to yarn hairiness; any observed reduction must be due to an unrelated, coincidental process change","Lower surface FRICTION coefficient of the ceramic coating (compared to standard steel) reduces the frictional drag/abrasion the yarn experiences as it rapidly slides through/around the traveller during spinning — since hairiness partly results from fiber ends being pulled/abraded loose by friction against machine surfaces (as established in the earlier yarn-friction discussion), a lower-friction traveller surface reduces this specific source of fiber-end disruption, directly reducing hairiness generation","Ceramic coating always INCREASES friction compared to steel, the opposite of the actual relationship that would explain a hairiness reduction","Traveller material composition has no relationship to yarn surface friction or fiber abrasion, making this observed hairiness improvement technically inexplicable through any surface-property mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Lower surface FRICTION coefficient of the ceramic coating (compared to standard steel) reduces the frictional drag/abrasion the yarn experiences as it rapidly slides through/around the traveller during spinning — since hairiness partly results from fiber ends being pulled/abraded loose by friction against machine surfaces (as established in the earlier yarn-friction discussion), a lower-friction traveller surface reduces this specific source of fiber-end disruption, directly reducing hairiness generation', 'This connects the earlier yarn-friction-coefficient discussion to a specific machine-material engineering choice — since the traveller is a high-speed contact point the yarn continuously slides against during spinning, its surface friction characteristics directly affect how much fiber-end abrasion/disruption occurs at that contact point; ceramic coatings typically offer measurably lower friction coefficients than standard steel, reducing this specific source of hairiness generation WITHOUT needing to change traveller weight (and therefore without affecting the tension-control function established earlier) — illustrating that traveller SURFACE MATERIAL and traveller WEIGHT are two independent design variables addressing two different yarn quality concerns (hairiness vs. tension/breakage).', 3, '{"traveller-material-hairiness"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A quality engineer investigating why COMBED yarn from Supplier A shows measurably better USTER evenness statistics than COMBED yarn from Supplier B, despite both suppliers using IDENTICAL raw cotton and IDENTICAL nominal combing noil%, discovers Supplier A uses a ''super lap'' preparation stage (combining multiple lap-forming passages) before combing, while Supplier B feeds combing directly from a simpler single-passage lap. How would this preparation difference plausibly explain the evenness gap?', '["Lap preparation method has no relationship to combed yarn evenness, making this observed quality gap between the two suppliers technically unexplainable through any preparation-stage difference","A ''super lap'' (multi-passage, more thoroughly doubled/blended lap preparation) provides MORE doubling/averaging opportunity (directly connecting to the earlier doubling-averaging-evenness principle from draw frame theory) before the material even reaches combing, giving the comb a more UNIFORM, better-prepared input lap to work with — a more thoroughly averaged input generally allows the downstream combing and subsequent processes to achieve better final evenness than starting from a less-doubled, simpler single-passage lap","Combing noil percentage is the ONLY factor that can ever affect combed yarn evenness, making any lap-preparation-stage explanation for this gap technically impossible","Super lap preparation always makes evenness WORSE compared to simple single-passage lap preparation, the opposite of the correct relationship between doubling/preparation thoroughness and evenness"]'::jsonb, 'A ''super lap'' (multi-passage, more thoroughly doubled/blended lap preparation) provides MORE doubling/averaging opportunity (directly connecting to the earlier doubling-averaging-evenness principle from draw frame theory) before the material even reaches combing, giving the comb a more UNIFORM, better-prepared input lap to work with — a more thoroughly averaged input generally allows the downstream combing and subsequent processes to achieve better final evenness than starting from a less-doubled, simpler single-passage lap', 'This directly applies the earlier draw-frame-doubling-averaging-evenness principle to a different process stage (lap preparation before combing) — a ''super lap'' process involves MORE doubling/combining passages before combing, giving MORE opportunity for the same statistical averaging effect (random irregularities in one input strand being compensated by others when combined) that makes draw frame doubling improve evenness — feeding combing (and all subsequent downstream processes) from this more thoroughly averaged, more uniform lap generally yields better final evenness than starting from a less-doubled, simpler preparation, even with identical raw cotton and identical noil% — illustrating that evenness quality is influenced by decisions made well BEFORE the specific process stage where the defect might be measured.', 3, '{"super-lap-evenness"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A textile engineer explains ''spinning triangle geometry'' more precisely: the triangle''s BASE WIDTH (how wide the drafted fiber strand is just before convergence into the yarn) directly affects hairiness, and compact spinning''s condensing zone reduces this base width. If a compact spinning system''s condensing (suction) zone becomes PARTIALLY BLOCKED/reduced in suction effectiveness (e.g., from lint buildup), what would be the expected quality consequence, and why might this be a SUBTLE, easily-missed maintenance issue?', '["A partially blocked condensing zone has no effect on yarn quality since the compact spinning mechanism is entirely independent of suction effectiveness, making this scenario technically impossible","Partial suction loss would progressively WIDEN the effective spinning triangle base back toward conventional (non-compact) dimensions, gradually INCREASING hairiness back toward conventional-spinning levels — this could be subtle/easily missed because the machine LOOKS like it''s still running in ''compact mode'' (no obvious visual/mechanical fault) while actually delivering degraded, less-effective compacting performance, requiring active hairiness monitoring (not just visual machine inspection) to catch","Suction effectiveness only affects the compact spinning system''s ENERGY consumption, with zero relationship to actual yarn hairiness or quality output","A partially blocked condensing zone would always cause an immediate, obvious, catastrophic machine shutdown, making this described ''subtle, easily-missed'' maintenance issue scenario technically implausible"]'::jsonb, 'Partial suction loss would progressively WIDEN the effective spinning triangle base back toward conventional (non-compact) dimensions, gradually INCREASING hairiness back toward conventional-spinning levels — this could be subtle/easily missed because the machine LOOKS like it''s still running in ''compact mode'' (no obvious visual/mechanical fault) while actually delivering degraded, less-effective compacting performance, requiring active hairiness monitoring (not just visual machine inspection) to catch', 'This directly extends the earlier compact-spinning/spinning-triangle-reduction discussion with a genuine maintenance-diagnostic insight — compact spinning''s hairiness benefit specifically comes from the condensing zone''s suction narrowing the spinning triangle base; if this suction gradually degrades (partial blockage from lint buildup, a common real-world maintenance issue), the effective triangle width creeps back toward conventional dimensions, progressively eroding the hairiness benefit — critically, this degradation can be MECHANICALLY subtle (the machine still visibly runs in ''compact mode,'' no obvious fault alarm) while the actual QUALITY OUTPUT silently degrades, meaning mills must actively monitor yarn hairiness statistics (not just assume compact mode = compact-quality results) to catch this specific gradual maintenance-related quality drift.', 3, '{"compact-spinning-maintenance-drift"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', '''Siro spinning'' feeds TWO separate roving strands (with a small physical gap between them) into the SAME ring frame drafting zone, which then twist together at the point of yarn formation, producing a single yarn with a ply-like, low-hairiness structure WITHOUT a separate plying/twisting machine step. Why does maintaining a precise GAP distance between the two roving strands matter for this technique to work correctly?', '["Gap distance has no effect on siro spinning''s outcome; any spacing (or none at all) between the two rovings produces identical results","The gap must be precisely calibrated so the two roving strands remain PARTIALLY SEPARATE through drafting (each forming its own mini spinning-triangle) before converging and twisting together at the final convergence point — too small a gap and the strands merge too early (behaving like a single, wider strand rather than two distinct plies, losing the ply-like structure benefit), while too large a gap risks the two strands failing to converge/twist together reliably, causing spinning instability or a split, incompletely-joined yarn","The gap exists purely for visual/cosmetic identification purposes on the machine, with zero effect on the actual yarn structure being formed","Siro spinning requires the two roving strands to be fed through completely separate, unconnected drafting zones on entirely different parts of the machine, making the concept of a single shared ''gap'' technically inapplicable"]'::jsonb, 'The gap must be precisely calibrated so the two roving strands remain PARTIALLY SEPARATE through drafting (each forming its own mini spinning-triangle) before converging and twisting together at the final convergence point — too small a gap and the strands merge too early (behaving like a single, wider strand rather than two distinct plies, losing the ply-like structure benefit), while too large a gap risks the two strands failing to converge/twist together reliably, causing spinning instability or a split, incompletely-joined yarn', 'This is a genuinely precise process-engineering detail specific to siro spinning technology — the deliberate gap between the two roving strands must be carefully calibrated: it needs to be wide enough that each strand maintains its own semi-independent drafting/mini-triangle behavior through most of the drafting zone (preserving the eventual TWO-PLY-LIKE structure, with its associated hairiness/strength benefits similar to true plied yarn), but narrow enough that the two strands reliably CONVERGE and twist together at the final point rather than exiting as two separate, unjoined strands — this gap calibration is a defining, precisely-tuned parameter of the siro spinning process, distinct from both conventional single-strand ring spinning and true two-stage ply-twisting.', 3, '{"siro-spinning-gap"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'Murata Vortex Spinning (MVS) uses a high-velocity AIR VORTEX (not false-twist, unlike air-jet texturing discussed earlier) to insert GENUINE, permanent twist into a fiber strand at very high production speed, positioned as an alternative to both ring and rotor spinning for certain applications. How does MVS''s air-vortex twisting mechanism fundamentally differ from air-jet TEXTURING''s false-twist mechanism discussed earlier in filament yarn processing?', '["MVS and air-jet texturing use mathematically identical mechanisms, making any distinction between them technically meaningless","Air-jet texturing (used on continuous FILAMENT yarn) inserts TEMPORARY (false) twist that is later removed, relying on heat-setting to lock in a permanent CRIMP rather than permanent twist itself — MVS instead uses air vortex forces to wrap and bind loose STAPLE fiber ends around a core in a way that creates GENUINE, PERMANENT structural twist/cohesion directly (no untwisting step, no heat-setting requirement for the twist itself), making it fundamentally a STAPLE-fiber SPINNING technology (competing with ring/rotor) rather than a filament TEXTURING technology","MVS can only process continuous filament yarn, never staple fiber, making it functionally identical in application scope to air-jet texturing","Air-jet texturing produces permanent twist while MVS produces only temporary, removable twist, the reverse of the actual relationship between these two distinct air-based technologies"]'::jsonb, 'Air-jet texturing (used on continuous FILAMENT yarn) inserts TEMPORARY (false) twist that is later removed, relying on heat-setting to lock in a permanent CRIMP rather than permanent twist itself — MVS instead uses air vortex forces to wrap and bind loose STAPLE fiber ends around a core in a way that creates GENUINE, PERMANENT structural twist/cohesion directly (no untwisting step, no heat-setting requirement for the twist itself), making it fundamentally a STAPLE-fiber SPINNING technology (competing with ring/rotor) rather than a filament TEXTURING technology', 'This is an important, easily-confused technology distinction since both use ''air'' as the twist-insertion mechanism but for fundamentally DIFFERENT purposes and fiber types — air-JET TEXTURING (discussed earlier) processes continuous FILAMENT and inserts only TEMPORARY false twist (removed after heat-setting locks in a crimp, not permanent twist), while MVS processes STAPLE fiber (like a ring or rotor spinning alternative) using air vortex forces to genuinely and PERMANENTLY wrap/bind fiber ends into a cohesive twisted yarn structure directly, with no untwisting step involved — despite the shared ''air'' terminology, these are fundamentally different technologies solving different problems (filament texture modification vs. staple fiber spinning) for different fiber input types.', 3, '{"MVS-vs-air-jet-texturing"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A drafting system engineer explains that the ''apron'' (a rubber or synthetic belt in the double-apron drafting zone of a draw frame/ring frame, positioned to guide and control fiber movement between the main drafting rollers) prevents a specific fiber-control problem that would occur with SIMPLE roller-pair drafting alone (no aprons). What problem does the apron solve?', '["Aprons serve no genuine fiber-control function and are purely a legacy design element with zero effect on drafting quality","Without apron control, fibers in the middle of the drafting zone (not yet fully gripped by either the back or front roller pair) have NO controlled guidance and can move somewhat randomly/prematurely relative to their neighbors — this ''floating fiber'' control problem causes uneven, unpredictable drafting (poor evenness) since fibers should ideally be released from the back zone''s control and accelerated by the front zone in a controlled, gradual manner; the apron extends controlled contact/guidance further into the drafting zone, reducing this floating-fiber irregularity and improving drafting evenness","Aprons exist solely to clean lint/dust off the rollers, with zero relationship to actual fiber drafting control or resulting yarn/sliver evenness","The apron''s only function is to physically prevent the two roller pairs from ever touching each other, with zero involvement in controlling fiber movement during the drafting process itself"]'::jsonb, 'Without apron control, fibers in the middle of the drafting zone (not yet fully gripped by either the back or front roller pair) have NO controlled guidance and can move somewhat randomly/prematurely relative to their neighbors — this ''floating fiber'' control problem causes uneven, unpredictable drafting (poor evenness) since fibers should ideally be released from the back zone''s control and accelerated by the front zone in a controlled, gradual manner; the apron extends controlled contact/guidance further into the drafting zone, reducing this floating-fiber irregularity and improving drafting evenness', 'This addresses the classic ''floating fiber control'' problem in drafting theory — between the back roller pair (initially gripping/holding fiber) and the front roller pair (accelerating fiber to final delivery speed), there''s a zone where SOME fibers are no longer gripped by the back rollers but not yet gripped by the front rollers — without additional guidance, these ''floating'' fibers can move somewhat unpredictably (prematurely accelerating or lagging relative to their intended, controlled release), directly causing drafting irregularity; the apron system extends light, controlled contact/guidance further into this zone, giving floating fibers more controlled, gradual release rather than abrupt, unguided movement, directly improving the resulting sliver/yarn evenness compared to simple roller-pair-only drafting.', 3, '{"apron-drafting-control"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A ring frame technologist explains that SMALLER ring diameter generally allows HIGHER maximum spindle speed (for the SAME traveller/tension conditions) compared to a larger ring diameter. Given the earlier balloon-control-ring/centrifugal-force discussion, why does ring diameter itself (independent of spindle RPM) affect the achievable speed limit?', '["Ring diameter has no relationship to achievable spindle speed or balloon dynamics, making this described relationship technically incorrect","For the SAME rotational speed (RPM), a LARGER ring diameter means the traveller (and the yarn balloon) travels at a higher LINEAR/tangential velocity (since linear velocity = radius × angular velocity) — since centrifugal force and air drag both scale with this linear velocity, a larger ring diameter reaches problematic tension/force levels at a LOWER RPM than a smaller ring diameter would, meaning smaller rings can be spun at higher RPM before hitting the same physical force/tension limits","Larger ring diameter always allows proportionally HIGHER maximum spindle speed than smaller ring diameter, the opposite of the actual physical relationship between ring size and speed limits","Ring diameter only affects the final bobbin''s maximum yarn storage capacity, with zero relationship to achievable spinning speed or balloon dynamics"]'::jsonb, 'For the SAME rotational speed (RPM), a LARGER ring diameter means the traveller (and the yarn balloon) travels at a higher LINEAR/tangential velocity (since linear velocity = radius × angular velocity) — since centrifugal force and air drag both scale with this linear velocity, a larger ring diameter reaches problematic tension/force levels at a LOWER RPM than a smaller ring diameter would, meaning smaller rings can be spun at higher RPM before hitting the same physical force/tension limits', 'This extends the earlier balloon-control-ring/centrifugal-force physics discussion with the ring-diameter variable made explicit — since linear (tangential) velocity = radius × angular velocity (RPM), a LARGER ring diameter means the traveller physically travels FASTER (in linear terms) at any given RPM than a smaller ring would — and since the destabilizing forces (centrifugal force, air drag on the balloon) scale with this LINEAR velocity, a larger ring reaches the same problematic force/tension thresholds at a LOWER RPM, meaning mills spinning at very high RPM for high productivity often deliberately choose SMALLER ring diameters specifically to push the achievable speed ceiling higher before hitting these balloon-dynamics limits, a genuine, deliberate machine-design tradeoff (smaller ring: higher achievable speed, but also smaller bobbin/less yarn storage capacity per doff).', 3, '{"ring-diameter-speed-limit"}'::text[]);
